        summary: ['Every circuit calculation starts with three rules. Ohm\'s law says the voltage across a resistor is the current through it times its resistance. Kirchhoff\'s current law (KCL) says the currents entering a node add up to the currents leaving it — charge is not stored in a wire. Kirchhoff\'s voltage law (KVL) says the voltages around any closed loop add up to zero — energy is conserved.', 'Resistors in series add; resistors in parallel combine as the reciprocal of the sum of reciprocals. Two resistors in parallel give R1·R2/(R1 + R2), which is always smaller than the smaller one.'],
        formulas: ['V = I·R', 'ΣI(in) = ΣI(out)  (KCL)', 'ΣV around a loop = 0  (KVL)', 'Series: R = R1 + R2', 'Parallel: R = R1·R2 / (R1 + R2)'],
