        summary: ['A bridge compares an unknown impedance with known ones. When the detector between the two middle points reads zero, the products of opposite arms are equal: Z1·Z4 = Z2·Z3. Because impedances are complex, both the magnitude and the angle must balance, so an AC bridge always needs two adjustments.', 'Maxwell\'s bridge measures a coil against a capacitor (good for medium-Q coils), Hay\'s bridge suits high-Q coils, Schering\'s bridge measures capacitors and their loss, and Wien\'s bridge balances at one frequency only, so it also measures frequency.'],
